From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Muthukumar <muthukumardce@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 29 May 2026 06:37:06 +0530
Subject: Blink: pin NetworkInformation::effectiveType to 4g to match forced
 rtt/downlink
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

navigator.connection was already overridden to report a fast desktop
profile when not observing change events: rtt()=50ms and downlink()=10Mbps.
However effectiveType() still deferred to the NetworkQualityEstimator, so a
slow proxy would drag the reported value to '3g' while rtt stayed pinned at
50ms.

That combination is internally contradictory — '3g' implies an RTT of
roughly >=270ms, not 50ms — and anti-bot scoring (e.g. Akamai) flags the
navigator.connection object as inconsistent. Akamai does not cross-check the
reported rtt against the real network latency (stock Chrome reports rtt:0/4g
through the same slow proxy and passes); it only checks that the connection
object is self-consistent.

Pin effectiveType() to k4G in the non-observing branch so the trio
(effectiveType=4g, rtt=50, downlink=10) presents a single coherent fast
profile matching what stock desktop Chrome reports through a slow proxy.
Update the rtt()/downlink() comments to document the pairing.

diff --git a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/netinfo/network_information.cc b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/netinfo/network_information.cc
index 92f01c147c5ee22a4371b8ddfd3b6413e4659d2d..4e35e19a5665eb676a949b5940127944fa289e6d 100644
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/netinfo/network_information.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/netinfo/network_information.cc
@@ -122,8 +122,11 @@ V8EffectiveConnectionType NetworkInformation::effectiveType() {
   // effective_type_ is only updated when listening for events, so ask
   // networkStateNotifier if not listening (crbug.com/379841).
   if (!IsObserving()) {
-    return V8EffectiveConnectionType(EffectiveConnectionTypeToEnum(
-        GetNetworkStateNotifier().EffectiveType()));
+    // Pin to 4g to MATCH the forced rtt()=50 / downlink()=10 fast profile.
+    // A slow proxy otherwise makes NQE report '3g' here while rtt stays 50,
+    // an internal contradiction anti-bot scoring flags. Stock Chrome shows a
+    // consistent 4g/low-rtt profile even through a slow proxy.
+    return V8EffectiveConnectionType(V8EffectiveConnectionType::Enum::k4G);
   }
 
   // If observing, return m_type which changes when the event fires, per spec.
@@ -141,8 +144,14 @@ uint32_t NetworkInformation::rtt() {
   }
 
   if (!IsObserving()) {
-    // Return a typical desktop Chrome RTT (50ms) instead of NQE 4G defaults
-    // (175ms→200ms) which are unusual for desktop browsers.
+    // Desktop Chrome reports a fast, internally-consistent profile (low rtt +
+    // effectiveType 4g) even through a slow proxy. Keep rtt at a realistic
+    // desktop 50ms; effectiveType() below is pinned to 4g to MATCH it. Without
+    // that pin, a slow proxy drags effectiveType to '3g' while rtt stays 50 —
+    // a self-contradiction (3g implies rtt >= ~270ms) that anti-bot scoring
+    // flags. Akamai does not cross-check rtt vs real latency (stock Chrome
+    // reports rtt:0/4g through the same slow proxy and passes); it checks that
+    // navigator.connection is self-consistent.
     return 50;
   }
 
@@ -159,7 +168,7 @@ double NetworkInformation::downlink() {
   }
 
   if (!IsObserving()) {
-    // Return a typical desktop Chrome downlink (10 Mbps) matching the RTT fix.
+    // Paired with rtt()=50 and effectiveType()=4g for a consistent fast profile.
     return 10.0;
   }
 
